  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \n\nA nor'easter is currently centered across southern New Jersey \nand will continue to sit and spin near the mid-Atlantic coast \nthough tonight. This storm has also begun its weakening stage \nwith its central pressure rising to near 1000 hPa based on the \nlatest RAP analysis. A band of rain continues to slowly lift \nnorthward and westward into areas north of I-90 with a well \npronounced dry slot bringing a brief dry period to areas mainly \nsouth of I-90. This dry slot will get cut off later this \nafternoon into tonight as another moisture plume and band of \nrain rotates around the primary low and reaches the area \ntonight and continues through much of tomorrow. So, expect \nperiods of rain to continue through Monday for much of the \nregion. The low will begin to track northeastward and out to \nsea by Monday night with precipitation ending across the region.\nAdditional rainfall amounts of 0.50 to 1.00 inches are expected\nfor many areas through tomorrow with up to 2 inches in the \neastern Catskills. With the continued long duration of this \nevent plus some dry periods and hourly rainfall rates mainly \n0.25 inches or less, no hydrological issues are expected.\n\nWith the weakening of the storm itself, the strongest winds from\nthis storm have ended. The wind advisory that was in effect for\nportions of the mid-Hudson Valley, southern Taconics, Litchfield\nCounty and Berkshire County was allowed to expire at noon. Wind\ngusts up to around 30 mph are expected through this afternoon \nbefore diminishing further tonight and through tomorrow. By \ntomorrow, wind gusts will only be around 20 mph.\n\nThe persistent clouds and occasional rain will keep high\ntemperatures below normal in the mid-50s to lower 60s through \ntomorrow. Partial clearing by Monday night along with light to \ncalm winds should allow for some fog formation.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nUpper level ridging and surface high pressure build across the\nregion on Tuesday and remains into Thursday with a period of\ndrier weather. Temperatures will slowly rebound back to near\nnormal levels on Wednesday and above normal levels on Thursday.\nThe next chance for rain showers looks to be on Friday as a cold\nfront approaches from the west.",
